.\build_part6.py:10:        "body": "The oldest evidence connecting the name Phra Narai (the Hindu deity Vishnu) to Takua Pa is the Khao Phra Narai (Phra Narai hill) inscription. The inscription database dates it to the 6th century AD (11th century BE) and identifies it as an inscription at the base of a Hindu deity statue of Phra Narai. State cultural sites data explains that Takua Pa or Takola has been a port town and multicultural trade center since ancient times. This background gives the Phra Narai story significance for local history.\n\nHowever, these sources mention the ancient town, inscriptions, and ancient statues. They do not state the year Phra Narai Park was built, do not name the creator of the replica statue, and documents stating when the worship ceremony began are not verified. Therefore, it is suggested, not settled, whether the 'beginning' of the ceremony venue falls in the governor era, the tin mining era, or any specific period."
.\build_part6.py:14:        "body": "Takua Pa changed its role from a coastal port and tin mining town to a historical community using cultural heritage for city activities. Community sources state that mining brought in many Chinese laborers and left economic, cultural, and culinary legacies in the town. But there is no specific evidence that this worship ceremony was ever transferred, abandoned, or directly tied to the mining industry.\n\nDuring periods with clear news data, the event venue is Phra Narai Park opposite the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, and the ceremony uses the replica statue of Phra Narai and deities following ancient Brahmin rituals. Found news reports do not mention the registration as an archaeological site, major renovations, or damage from the 2004 AD tsunami for the park and replica statue, so these events should not be assumed over evidence."
.\build_part6.py:18:        "body": "The location is still used as a public park and town activity space, managed by the Takua Pa Town Municipality. The worship ceremony is held as an annual event according to found news. In 2023 AD, the event was scheduled for 27 March to 5 April, featuring local performances, OTOP products, local food, Red-whiskered bulbul singing contests, and competition activities.\n\nFor 2026 AD, an announced schedule from discovered sources is not verified. It can only be confirmed that the location is a public space and still plays a role in hosting events. Opening hours, admission fees, specific structural conditions, parking, restrooms, wheelchair accessibility, or current renovation projects are not verified."
.\build_part6.py:64:      "Direct image file URLs with verifiable usage rights are not verified, so images have not been added yet; using images from source pages requires prior permission."
.\build_part6.py:73:        "body": "Takua Pa has its roots as a riverside port town and a trading center with people from many nations before Thai communities came to mine tin. Later, when the original town was destroyed in 1809 AD (2352 BE) and people scattered, the town was revived at Ban Talat Yai (the large market village) during the reign of King Rama III. This began with Wat Na Mueang (the front town temple), followed by the construction of the governor's residence nearby. Verifiable sources do not yet specify the starting year of construction or the individual builder of the residence, so the current ceremony should not be directly tied to the residence's construction year.\n\nDuring the era when Takua Pa grew from tin mining, particularly since the reign of King Rama V, the town served as an administrative center, water transportation hub, and coastal trade center. Chinese communities from Fujian and Guangdong arrived as mine laborers and merchants, creating networks of Thai-Chinese or Baba families. The governor's residence was thus an administrative space and a symbol of the town's authority, rather than a place for performing worship ceremonies according to currently found evidence."
.\build_part6.py:77:        "body": "When the waterways became shallow and unnavigable, Ban Talat Yai lost its importance. In 1913 AD, the town center moved to Ban Yan Yao (the long neighborhood village) in Takua Pa subdistrict. The former residence area changed from an administrative center to a trace of the old town. Sources do not mention the transfer of ownership or other continuous uses of the residence. Evidence confirming that the residence was damaged by the 2004 AD tsunami is not verified.\n\nWhat clearly remains is the rectangular layout wall, dividing the area into the front palace, main hall, and rear palace. The Fine Arts Department announced the registration of the Takua Pa governor's residence wall and designated the archaeological site area in the Royal Gazette on 18 December 1996 AD. Current sources state that restorations and landscaping have occurred, but do not provide the year of the restoration work, so a year cannot be assigned."
.\build_part6.py:109:        "text": "Specific routes from Phuket in readable sources are not verified."
.\build_part6.py:113:        "text": "Specific routes from Khao Lak in readable sources are not verified."
.\build_part6.py:117:        "text": "Routes from the airport in readable sources are not verified."
.\build_part6.py:140:      "Distances from Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport with readable reference sources are not verified.",
